<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lü Guang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/337">337</a>–400</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Yiwu of Later Liang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lü <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> was a Di general of Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> whom the emperor Fu Jian sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in 382. He took Kucha in 384 and carried off the Buddhist monk and translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a>. When Former Qin collapsed after the Battle of the Fei River, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> in 386, taking the title Heavenly King in 396. He died in 400.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-guang.jpg" alt="Lü Guang" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jin, Shengtan, 1608-1661, creator Chen shi Chihuo zhai, ping shi. Guang bai song zhai 金人瑞, 1608-1661 陳氏尺蠖齋, 評釋. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BC_Guang_%E5%91%82%E5%85%89.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most famous monk, Kumārajīva (344–412/5), was captured when <b>Lü Guang</b> of the Later Liang attacked Kucha in 384, learned Chinese in captivity and from 401 headed a translation bureau at Chang’an.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Kucha's monasteries, including the painted caves at Kizil, made it a leading centre of Buddhism, and the translator Kumarajiva was born there in 344; in 384 the Former Qin general <b>Lü Guang</b> took the city and carried him east.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Kingdom of Kucha</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lü Guang</b>, a Di general of Former Qin, conquered Kucha and the Tarim kingdoms in 384.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang (386-403)</a></li></ul></section>
